          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "```json\n{\n  \"idea_id\": \"B09\",\n  \"payer\": {\n    \"who\": \"The private practice physician or practice owner (per-provider SaaS subscription, typically paid by the practice)\",\n    \"price_hypothesis\": \"$49–$99 per provider per month for a note-drafting/intake tool, anchored to ClaireMed's published $99/provider/month price\",\n    \"comparable_url\": \"https://clairemed.ai/faq\"\n  },\n  \"competitors\": [\n    { \"name\": \"ClaireMed (Claire)\", \"url\": \"https://clairemed.ai/\", \"pricing\": \"$99 per provider per month (one page also cites $499/mo or $4,999/yr for a different tier)\", \"pricing_url\": \"https://clairemed.ai/faq\" },\n    { \"name\": \"S10.AI\", \"url\": \"https://s10.ai/\", \"pricing\": \"$99–$500 per provider per month; enterprise plans $5,000–$20,000/year\", \"pricing_url\": \"https://s10.ai/blog/ai-scribe-cost\" },\n    { \"name\": \"AlloSylvia (Sylvia)\", \"url\": \"https://allosylvia.ai/en/\", \"pricing\": \"Not published; free tier only for students/residents, clinic pricing requires contact\", \"pricing_url\": \"https://allosylvia.ai/en/\" },\n    { \"name\": \"Practice Copilot (Patient Square)\", \"url\": \"https://patientsquare.com/us/products/practice-copilot/\", \"pricing\": \"Not published on site\", \"pricing_url\": \"https://patientsquare.com/us/products/practice-copilot/\" },\n    { \"name\": \"MayaMD\", \"url\": \"https://mayamd.ai/clinical-ai-agent\", \"pricing\": \"Not published\", \"pricing_url\": \"https://mayamd.ai/clinical-ai-agent\" },\n    { \"name\": \"Cydoc (defunct, direct precedent)\", \"url\": \"https://glassboxmedicine.com/2026/02/21/why-i-shut-down-my-bootstrapped-health-ai-startup-after-7-years-a-founders-postmortem/\", \"pricing\": \"Subscription priced near hosting cost; $4,200 of $27,900 total 2024 revenue came from subscriptions\", \"pricing_url\": \"https://glassboxmedicine.com/2026/02/21/why-i-shut-down-my-bootstrapped-health-ai-startup-after-7-years-a-founders-postmortem/\" }\n  ],\n  \"competitors_not_found_queries\": [],\n  \"acquisition_channel\": [\n    {\n      \"channel\": \"Exhibiting at primary-care/specialty medical conferences (booth + materials) — the channel the closest direct precedent (Cydoc) actually used\",\n      \"cost_estimate\": \"$3,000–$10,000 for a 10x10 booth at a mid-size medical conference, all-in (space is only 40-60% of total cost); smaller shows like AAFP FUTURE start near $1,850 for space alone\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.buzzboxmedia.com/guides/medical-conference-costs/\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"demand_evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"quote\": \"I asked my employer to add more intake paperwork before visits due to 40-minute slots but they never followed up. Frustrating.\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.reddit.com/r/PMHNP/comments/1jywdiz/job_is_okay_with_patients_not_filling_out_intake/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"quote\": \"1 in 5 physicians spend eight-plus hours a week in the EHR after hours... charting from the couch, signing from bed.\",\n      \"url\": \"https://patientsquare.com/us/products/practice-copilot/\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"case_for\": \"The pain is real and well documented (AMA burnout data cited by competitors, physician forum complaints, a $1T administrative-cost framing for intake/documentation), and multiple funded competitors (ClaireMed, S10.AI, Practice Copilot, MayaMD, AlloSylvia) are already charging $99–$500/provider/month and finding some traction, proving willingness to pay exists. A solo builder with AI agents could scope this far narrower than Cydoc did: skip building a new EHR, skip mobile apps, and ship a thin layer that only does intake conversation + note draft that pastes/exports into the physician's existing EHR (the same positioning Practice Copilot and ClaireMed now use). At ClaireMed's $99/mo price point, even 20-30 solo/small-practice physicians recruited via content, direct outreach, or a single conference presence would generate $2-3k MRR, which is plausible revenue for a one-person AI-run company with near-zero marginal cost, even if it never becomes venture-scale.\",\n  \"case_against\": \"This is not a hypothetical risk — it is a documented direct precedent. Cydoc, built by an MD/PhD founder over seven years, was almost exactly this idea (automated history-taking that generates a note for private practice physicians, HIPAA-compliant, targeting the same customer). It reached only four paying customers, $27,900 in total 2024 revenue (most from a report-generation service, not the core subscription), a per-practice integration cost of $4,000-$6,000, and an estimated 11 years to break even per practice — and the founder shut it down after seven years, writing that 'deploying health AI is only 20% of the challenge.' Her own first sales call revealed the core problem: the physician only wanted part of the workflow (HPI drafting) and had no interest in the rest, meaning product scope had to be renegotiated per customer. Six weeks is not enough time to solve what took a domain-expert MD/PhD founder years to even partially solve: EHR-specific integration, BAA execution and ongoing HIPAA obligations, physician trust in AI-drafted notes they are liable for, and a sales cycle that in the precedent case ran over a year per customer. The niche is also now crowded with better-resourced direct competitors (ClaireMed, MayaMD, AlloSylvia, S10.AI, Practice Copilot) all pitching nearly identical value propositions, which raises the acquisition cost and lowers the odds a single operator differentiates enough to win deals from physicians who are already being pitched by these vendors.\",\n  \"open_questions\": [\n    \"Can the product be scoped as a pure 'add-on that pastes into the existing EHR' (like Practice Copilot/ClaireMed) to avoid Cydoc's costly per-practice integration work, and does that actually get integration cost below $500/practice?\",\n    \"What is the realistic sales-cycle length and CAC for one physician-owner in a private practice, given the direct precedent took a year+ per customer relationship?\",\n    \"Do physicians actually want the patient to answer AI intake questions before the visit, or (per Cydoc's own first customer) do they only want the after-the-fact note draft — which changes both the product and the liability/regulatory surface?\",\n    \"What is the real 12-month retention rate for a $99/mo competitor like ClaireMed — do practices keep paying, or does workflow friction push them out as it did for Cydoc's four customers?\",\n    \"Is there a narrower specialty or workflow (e.g., psych intake reports, which generated most of Cydoc's actual revenue) where willingness to pay is meaningfully higher than generic primary-care intake?\"\n  ],\n  \"regulatory_setup\": {\n    \"obligations\": [\n      {\n        \"obligation\": \"Execute a HIPAA Business Associate Agreement (BAA) with every covered-entity customer (physician practice) before handling any PHI\",\n        \"kind\": \"ongoing\",\n        \"consult_scope\": \"One-time attorney review/drafting of a standard BAA template and data-flow assessment, then reused per customer without new legal work unless terms are negotiated\",\n        \"source_url\": \"https://www.trytwofold.com/blog/do-you-need-a-baa-for-ai-notes\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"obligation\": \"Maintain HIPAA technical/administrative safeguards: encryption in transit and at rest, access logging, least-privilege access\",\n        \"kind\": \"ongoing\",\n        \"consult_scope\": \"Covered by the same initial compliance consult; ongoing engineering work rather than recurring legal work\",\n        \"source_url\": \"https://clairemed.ai/faq\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"obligation\": \"Clarify product is a documentation/workflow tool and not a regulated medical device (no diagnosis/treatment claims) to stay outside FDA device regulation\",\n        \"kind\": \"one_time\",\n        \"consult_scope\": \"One-time legal/regulatory consult to review marketing claims and product scope against FDA's clinical decision support / device guidance\",\n        \"source_url\": \"https://clairemed.ai/faq\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"unsourced_claims\": [],\n  \"injection_seen\": [],\n  \"brief_md\": \"## B09 — Smart AI Patient Intake & Note Generator for Private Practice Physicians\\n\\n### Payer\\nThe paying customer is the private practice physician or practice owner, buying a per-provider SaaS subscription. Price hypothesis: **$49–$99/provider/month**, anchored to ClaireMed's published $99/provider/month ([source](https://clairemed.ai/faq)).\\n\\n### Competitors\\n- **ClaireMed** — $99/provider/month, HIPAA/PHIPA/PIPEDA compliant, signs BAAs. ([faq](https://clairemed.ai/faq))\\n- **S10.AI** — $99–$500/provider/month, enterprise $5,000–$20,000/yr. ([pricing](https://s10.ai/blog/ai-scribe-cost))\\n- **AlloSylvia**, **Practice Copilot**, **MayaMD** — direct/adjacent competitors, pricing not publicly listed (contact-sales models).\\n- **Cydoc (defunct)** — the closest direct precedent; not a live competitor but essential evidence (see Case Against).\\n\\n### Acquisition Channel\\nConference exhibiting (the channel the closest precedent, Cydoc, actually used per its own postmortem photo caption) costs **$3,000–$10,000 all-in for a 10x10 booth** at a mid-size medical conference, with cheaper options (~$1,850 space-only) at smaller shows like AAFP FUTURE. ([Buzzbox Media](https://www.buzzboxmedia.com/guides/medical-conference-costs/))\\n\\n### Demand Evidence\\n- A PMHNP describes trying and failing to get more intake paperwork adopted, ending in frustration: *\\\"I also asked my place of employment to incorporate more paperwork for patients before intakes due to 40 minute time slots but they never followed up... Frustrating.\\\"* ([Reddit](https://www.reddit.com/r/PMHNP/comments/1jywdiz/job_is_okay_with_patients_not_filling_out_intake/))\\n- Competitor marketing (citing AMA data) notes **1 in 5 physicians spend 8+ hours/week in the EHR after hours**. ([Practice Copilot](https://patientsquare.com/us/products/practice-copilot/))\\n\\n### Case For\\nThe administrative pain is real and quantified, and multiple funded competitors already charge $99–$500/provider/month with apparent traction, proving willingness to pay. A solo builder with AI agents could scope this far more narrowly than the direct precedent (Cydoc) did — skip building a custom EHR, ship only an intake-conversation-to-note-draft layer that pastes into the physician's existing system — and even a small base of 20-30 subscribing solo physicians would generate meaningful MRR for a zero-marginal-cost, agent-run operation.\\n\\n### Case Against\\nThis exact idea has a documented seven-year failure case: **Cydoc**, built by an MD/PhD founder, was an AI-driven automated history-taking tool generating notes for private practice physicians — nearly identical to B09. It reached only four paying customers, $27,900 total revenue in 2024 (most of it non-subscription), a $4,000–$6,000 per-practice integration cost, an estimated 11-year break-even per practice, and shut down after the founder concluded that \\\"deploying health AI is only 20% of the challenge.\\\" Her first real sales call showed customers want to renegotiate scope per practice (the physician only wanted the note-drafting piece, not the full history workflow), foreshadowing a long, custom sales cycle. Six weeks cannot replicate what took a clinically credentialed founder years to partially solve: EHR integration, BAA/HIPAA overhead, physician trust in liability-bearing AI notes, and a slow per-practice sales cycle — all while several better-funded direct competitors already occupy this exact niche.\\n\\n### Open Questions\\nSee `open_questions` field — chiefly whether integration cost can be driven far below Cydoc's $4-6k/practice, what CAC and sales-cycle length look like for a solo operator, and whether a narrower specialty (e.g., psych reports, which generated most of Cydoc's actual revenue) is more tractable than general primary care.\\n\\n### Regulatory Setup\\nHIPAA BAA execution (ongoing, per customer), maintaining technical/administrative safeguards (ongoing), and clarifying non-device status to avoid FDA scope creep (one-time legal review). See `regulatory_setup` field for sources.\\n\"\n}\n```"
